<h2 id="seasonal">Seasonal side gigs in Chicago</h2>
<p>Chicago has two economies: summer and everything else. Smart hustlers plan for both.</p>
<div class="hustle-grid">
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="parking-rental">9. Renting your parking spot</h3>
    <p>If you have a garage or pad near Wrigley Field, the United Center, Soldier Field or the South Side ballpark, list it on SpotHero or a neighborhood group for game days and concerts. Downtown residents with an unused deeded spot can rent it monthly.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $20–$50 per event, more for monthly</li><li><b>Startup:</b> $0</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Homeowners near venues</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="moving-help">10. Moving help</h3>
    <p>Walk-up apartments and narrow gangways make Chicago moves miserable, which is exactly why people pay. Dolly, TaskRabbit and Facebook groups post jobs constantly in late April and late August.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> Typically $30–$50/hr</li><li><b>Startup:</b> $0–$80 for a hand truck</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Strong backs</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="window-boxes">11. Balcony, rooftop and window-box gardening</h3>
    <p>Condo owners in Lincoln Park and the Gold Coast pay for spring planting and fall cleanup of balconies and roof decks. It is short, seasonal and often comes with referrals to the whole building.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $50–$200 per visit</li><li><b>Startup:</b> $50–$150</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Plant lovers</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="tour-guide">12. Walking and food tour guide</h3>
    <p>Architecture, food and history tours run from spring through fall. Experienced guides list on Airbnb Experiences and GetYourGuide or work with existing tour companies in the Loop, Pilsen and Chinatown.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> Typically $25–$50/hr plus tips</li><li><b>Startup:</b> $0</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Storytellers</li></ul>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 id="skills">Skill-based side gigs for Chicago professionals</h2>
<p>If you work in finance, healthcare, law or tech, your highest-paying side hustle is probably the skill you already use at work.</p>
<div class="hustle-grid">
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="tutoring">13. Tutoring and test prep</h3>
    <p>Selective enrollment high school testing, the SAT and college applications create steady demand from families on the North Side and in suburbs like Evanston, Wilmette and Naperville. Grad students at UChicago, Northwestern, Loyola, DePaul and UIC can tutor in person or online.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> Typically $30–$80/hr</li><li><b>Startup:</b> $0–$50</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Teachers, grad students</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="bookkeeping">14. Bookkeeping and virtual assistance</h3>
    <p>Restaurants, contractors and real estate teams across Chicagoland need help with invoices, scheduling and QuickBooks cleanup. Tax season is the busiest stretch.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> Typically $25–$50/hr</li><li><b>Startup:</b> Laptop</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Organized multitaskers</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="photography">15. Real estate and headshot photography</h3>
    <p>Chicago's condo market moves year-round, so agents always need listing photos. Professionals in the Loop also book headshots for LinkedIn and company sites.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $125–$300 per shoot</li><li><b>Startup:</b> Camera or recent phone</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Visual people</li></ul>
  </div>
