<h2 id="start-this-month">How to make money in Chicago starting this month</h2>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Pick one hustle and one area.</strong> A single neighborhood keeps travel time low and referrals tight. Not sure which fits? Try the <a href="/tools/side-hustle-quiz/">side hustle quiz</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Set a monthly target.</strong> Work backward from the money you need to the hours you can commit.</li>
  <li><strong>Get listed where Chicagoans look.</strong> Gig apps for driving and delivery, Nextdoor and neighborhood Facebook groups for local services, and a free Google Business Profile once you have a few clients.</li>
  <li><strong>Mark the surge calendar.</strong> Home games, Lollapalooza, the Marathon, big McCormick Place shows and the first big snowfall.</li>
  <li><strong>Collect reviews early.</strong> Five strong reviews beat any ad budget.</li>
  <li><strong>Separate your money.</strong> Open a dedicated account and log every mile and receipt from day one.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="illinois-taxes">Illinois taxes and Chicago rules for side hustlers</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>State income tax:</strong> Illinois has a flat 4.95% individual income tax on your net profit.</li>
  <li><strong>No city income tax:</strong> Chicago does not tax personal income, so you avoid the municipal income taxes found in Ohio and Detroit.</li>
  <li><strong>Self-employment tax:</strong> 15.3% federal on net profit, on top of federal income tax. Estimate your set-aside with the <a href="/tools/self-employment-tax-calculator/">self-employment tax calculator</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Sales tax:</strong> if you sell physical products, register with the Illinois Department of Revenue. The combined rate inside Chicago is 10.25% or higher, among the steepest in the country.</li>
  <li><strong>Licenses:</strong> Chicago's Department of Business Affairs and Consumer Protection handles business licenses. Short-term rental units must be registered with the city before they are advertised, and food and home-repair businesses have their own rules.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout callout-warn"><strong>Watch out:</strong> Listing a Chicago apartment on Airbnb without an approved city registration can lead to fines, and many leases and condo boards ban short-term rentals outright. Check both before you list.</div>

